Students with dyslexia, ADHD, dyscalculia, or high anxiety perform significantly below their actual ability on timed standardised tests, producing false negatives that exclude capable students.
Average SAT scores differ by $20,000 family income brackets — not because intelligence differs by income, but because expensive test prep ($1,000–$2,000 courses) systematically improves scores.
Claude Steele's stereotype threat research shows that reminding Black students of their race before a test reduces performance by 8–10 points — the testing context itself introduces bias unrelated to ability.
